Q: How to have separate ContentSecurityPolicy.php for different environments in CI4? A: <p>For CSP settings in CI4 you may set in the app/Config/ContentSecurityPolicy.php by using a <code>conditional statement</code>, such as</p>
<p>Put inside the <code>public function __construct()</code> block</p>
<pre><code>if (ENVIRONMENT === 'development') {
            $this-&gt;reportOnly = true;
// other statements
        }
</code></pre>

Q: @react-native-firebase/app-check configuration always generates Unverified: invalid requests A: <p>However, it a very late response but perhaps you need to improve the code for logging. It should be something like below.</p>
<pre><code>    appCheck
      .initializeAppCheck({
        provider: rnfbProvider,
        isTokenAutoRefreshEnabled: true,
      })
      .then(() =&gt; console.log('App Check initialized successfully'))
      .catch((error) =&gt; console.error('Error initializing App Check:', error));
  }, []);
</code></pre>

Q: Code Completion on emplace_back in Visual Studio Pro A: <p>Sloppy speaking, <code>emplace_back</code> is not defined to have overloads for each constructor that the type has. But rather its a variadic template that first accepts any combinations of types of arguments. Only after the types have been deduced it is checked whether there is a constructor that matches. If you take into account possible implicit conversions, the set of instantiations of <code>emplace_back</code> that can be used to forward the arguments to a constructor is infinite.</p>
<p>For example</p>
<pre><code>struct foo { foo(int x); };
</code></pre>
<p>There is not one overload <code>std::vector&lt;foo&gt;::emplace_back(int)</code> that you wish to see, but <code>emplace_back(4.2)</code> instantiates <code>emplace_back&lt;double&gt;</code>, <code>emplace_back(uint8_t{42})</code> instantiates <code>emplace_back&lt;uint8_t&gt;</code>, etc., and they all have different signature yet call the same constructor.</p>
<p>The set of possible instantations is potentially infinte, if you consider that this works too:</p>
<pre><code>struct bar {
    operator int() {return 42;}
};

std::vector&lt;foo&gt; v;
v.emplace_back(bar{});
</code></pre>
<p>Note that this instantiates <code>std::vector&lt;foo&gt;::emplace_back&lt;bar&gt;</code>. It would be difficult to scan for all types that can be converted to <code>foo</code> to show it to you as overload canditate. I also don't think it would be very useful.</p>
<p>PS: I got the reference-ness wrong on the examples, but it doesn't affect the point I am trying to make.</p>
